{"author":{"id":"694fb8161bd8bf91df2a28db7c649e48f8c5fa47ad3ad0234607d35f0482801a"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790356683,"nonce":"HTZuIaN2UyQlyf6ZXhtDEIjndoA=","title":"A loop that only calls host constructors stays in its cheap baseline","message":"Caribou's interop bench (crates/caribou-interop/benches/interop.rs, cell \"construct\", column Wren→Haxe) loops in Wren over `Haxe.new()` through a foreign class. Built against this checkout at 196eca0 (tiered mode), the cell costs 180–570 ns per call against 77 at 5b03831b; the same loop constructing a Wren class costs 31 ns tiered against 154 interpreted, but the Haxe one costs 224 tiered against 292 interpreted, so tiering buys it almost nothing.\n\nA sample of the cell is led by vm_interp::build_arg_vals and run_fiber_loop, then call_native_with_frame_sync and call_host_with_frame_sync, with ExecutionEngine::propose_top_tier present.\n\nTwo of today's commits fit: c3da909 (a body whose loops only call is not proposed to LLVM) and 3994724 (a baseline body the top tier will replace is compiled cheaply). The bench's body is exactly a loop that only calls, so it may now keep the cheap baseline that expected to be replaced.\n\nTo reproduce: in caribou, `cargo bench -p caribou-interop --bench interop -- --only construct --column 3`, and with `--wren interpreter` for the comparison.","files":null}]}